<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ziggurat">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ziggurat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A ziggurat is a massive stepped temple tower built in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, with a shrine on its summit. Ziggurats were constructed of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud brick</a>, often faced with baked brick, and served as the centre of a city's religious life. The great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur">ziggurat of Ur</a> was built under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>, and the Elamite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/untash-napirisha">Untash-Napirisha</a> built the ziggurat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chogha-zanbil">Chogha Zanbil</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ziggurat.jpg" alt="Ziggurat" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: tobeytravels · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_Temple_ziggurat_in_Uruk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ishtar Gate, the Processional Way, the rebuilt temple of Marduk with its <b>ziggurat</b> Etemenanki, the South Palace and the Hanging Gardens, whose existence is still debated, all reflect a state channelling surplus revenue into monumental architecture with deliberate ideological intent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The construction of the Chogha Zanbil <b>ziggurat</b> complex under Untash-Napirisha in the thirteenth century BCE, a structure some 105 metres square at its base and now a UNESCO World Heritage Site, demonstrates the mobilisation of enormous surplus resources and a deliberate policy of religious inclusivity, with temples to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The treasures are now divided between the British Museum, the Penn Museum and the Baghdad Museum, and the partly restored <b>ziggurat</b>, built by Ur-Nammu and repaired by Ishme-Dagan of Isin, Kurigalzu I and Nabonidus, remains the best-preserved landmark of the site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Each god lived in a temple in its city, often a stepped <b>ziggurat</b>, present in a cult image to which banquets were served twice a day and before which incense was burned.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesopotamian-religion">Mesopotamian religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Early Dynastic period Eridu had lost political weight to Ur, though it remained a revered cult centre; Ur-Nammu and his successors of the Neo-Sumerian Empire built a <b>ziggurat</b> there.</blockquote><a href="/state/eridu">Eridu</a></li><li><blockquote>Kurigalzu I built a new royal city, Dur-Kurigalzu, with a <b>ziggurat</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kassite-babylonia">Kassite Babylonia</a></li></ul></section>
